WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.882
NULL values require special attention in aggregation
In the admissions

00:00:05.908 --> 00:00:10.687
table an ongoing admission has no discharge time yet
That means we

00:00:10.713 --> 00:00:16.571
cannot calculate a completed length of stay for that
row AVG ignores

00:00:16.598 --> 00:00:21.482
NULL expressions rather than treating them as zero
That behavior is useful

00:00:21.508 --> 00:00:26.517
here because an unfinished stay is not a zero-day
stay Run the

00:00:26.543 --> 00:00:32.217
example and look at the completed length-of-stay
calculation Think about which admissions

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:38.073
actually contribute to the average Whenever you
report an average also

00:00:38.099 --> 00:00:42.957
think about the sample size An average based on
eight completed admissions

00:00:42.984 --> 00:00:46.536
means something different from an average based on
all ten admission rows

00:00:47.457 --> 00:00:50.984
Missing values can change which records are included
in the measure
